One-shot cheat sheet (copy/paste)
# Terminal 1 — backend (default :8000; for :8001 set APP_PORT=8001 in .env — see Notes)
cd ~/projects/vendorpulse && docker compose up -d --build
# Terminal 2 — web + mobile web
cd ~/projects/vendorpulse && npm run dev:frontdoor
# Terminal 3 (optional) — Flutter native
cd ~/projects/vendorpulse/mobile_dashboard && flutter run --dart-define=VP_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1





Notes
Do not run npm run dev:api while Docker vendorpulse-app is on port 8000 — the API is already there.
After changing Dockerfile (IMAP), rebuild PHP containers:
  docker compose build --no-cache app horizon scheduler && docker compose up -d
Verify IMAP inside the API container (must print "imap" and "yes"):
  docker compose exec app php -m | grep -i imap
  docker compose exec app php -r "echo function_exists('imap_open') ? 'yes' : 'no';"
Experience monitoring (VAPT / Playwright) needs Node + Chromium on the Horizon worker:
  npm run experience-monitoring:install          # host-only Horizon
  docker compose exec horizon node -v
  docker compose exec horizon npx playwright --version
After Dockerfile changes, rebuild horizon too:
  docker compose build --no-cache horizon && docker compose up -d horizon
If the dashboard points at production, rebuild/restart production the same way — host `php artisan serve` without ext-imap will show the same error.
Ensure web_dashboard/.env.local has (match APP_PORT in root .env):
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1
For port 8001 instead: APP_PORT=8001 in .env and NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/v1
Stop everything: docker compose down and Ctrl+C in the frontend terminals.
